  "description": "The fourth-iteration review, and it found more than the loop had. Three of its factual claims were checked against the code before anything was acted on, as LOOP.md step 9 requires, and all three held -- two of them correcting things this loop had itself asserted.\n\nThe first is decision/0028. Issue 121 said Laravel's SQLite transaction mode could not be confirmed without vendor/, which cannot be installed here. It is stated at config/database.php:69, shipped by the Laravel 13 skeleton, and PR 94 -- \"state the embedded SQLite pragmas instead of inheriting them\" -- rewrote the block three lines above it and never mentioned it. So the run 0013 pragma work was half a fix and the missing half was on the same screen, and sqlite-busy-timeout-stated's why cites the jobs-table lock as something the stated timeout already covers. That drops issue 121 from an architectural choice to an S.\n\nThe second is decision/0027, and it is worse because it was repeated. \"No request, no error\" has been the standing description of issue 106 -- in the issue, in a comment, in decision/0026, and in reports. container-smoke.mjs carries no page.on() listeners of any kind and never has: no request, no console message, nothing. What was actually observed is that no files row appears and the form's button is left disabled. The absence of a request is a guess about the cause that has been steering every workaround, and it points diagnosis at \"the request never left the browser\" when the evidence fits equally a request that was made and answered with its state lost. Three bugs have been treated as one.\n\nThe third is issue 74, open since midday with no ledger node and never referenced by an item: Download redirects the browser to the presigned host, which for embedded storage defaults to 127.0.0.1:9000 -- the user's own machine, on a port the single-container run does not publish. The smoke never follows a Download link, so this is run/0003's shape again, a green fourteen-check CI with the feature dead in the product. It is also the design input item/api-content and item/api-presigned-upload both need, because spec 11's \"bytes never pass through PHP\" and spec 13's \"one container, one port\" collide there.\n\ndecision/0029 puts all three into the backlog at orders 23 to 25 rather than taking them as interruptions, so step 4 picks them by its own rule. The argument against -- that the loop has spent three of its last four runs off-backlog and the person proposing the detour is the one paying its cost -- is real and was put to the review deliberately. It loses on the observation that item/v1-audit is a verification item one place before the tag with no slack to fix anything, so deferring to it schedules a surprise rather than the work.\n\nThe review also named a structural blind spot worth recording plainly: the orchestrator cannot execute anything here, so it substitutes reading for running and then counts the reading as review. That fails reliably on one class -- over-determination, where two mechanisms produce the same observable -- which is exactly what the three false mutation proofs of runs 0014 and 0015 were, and what the two corrections above are. Its remedies are not yet taken and are recorded here rather than claimed: make the guards job's verdict the only admissible evidence for a mutation entry, have refusal tests assert WHICH guard refused rather than that something returned 403, and ship vendor/ into the session as a CI artifact so framework claims stop being arguments."
}
